    {
      "id": "c11-l1",
      "title": "Strings Are Arrays",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "C has no special string type. A string is an array of chars that ends with a zero byte, '\\0'. char word[] = \"unix\"; is really {'u', 'n', 'i', 'x', '\\0'}: five chars.",
        "The '\\0' marks the end. printf with %s prints characters until it finds it. Forget it, and printf keeps printing whatever is in memory after the string.",
        "Because a string is an array, word[0] is its first letter, and you can change single letters: word[0] = 'U';"
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    char word[] = \"unix\";",
          "    printf(\"%s\\n\", word);",
          "    printf(\"%c\\n\", word[1]);",
          "    word[0] = 'U';",
          "    printf(\"%s\\n\", word);",
          "    printf(\"%d bytes\\n\", (int) sizeof(word));",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "unix",
          "n",
          "Unix",
          "5 bytes"
        ]
      },
