    {
      "id": "c11-l2",
      "title": "Walking Through a String",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "To look at every character, loop until you reach the '\\0': for (i = 0; s[i] != '\\0'; i++).",
        "Letters are numbers, so you can test and change them: s[i] >= 'a' && s[i] <= 'z' finds lowercase letters, and s[i] - 32 turns one into uppercase ('a' is 97, 'A' is 65).",
        "This is how Unix tools like tr worked: walk through the text one character at a time."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    char s[] = \"hello, world\";",
          "    int i, vowels = 0;",
          "    for (i = 0; s[i] != '\\0'; i++) {",
          "        if (s[i] == 'a' || s[i] == 'e' || s[i] == 'i' || s[i] == 'o' || s[i] == 'u')",
          "            vowels++;",
          "        if (s[i] >= 'a' && s[i] <= 'z')",
          "            s[i] = s[i] - 32;",
          "    }",
          "    printf(\"%s\\n\", s);",
          "    printf(\"%d vowels\\n\", vowels);",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "HELLO, WORLD",
          "3 vowels"
        ]
      },
