    {
      "id": "c11-l3",
      "title": "The String Library",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "#include <string.h> brings ready-made string functions. strlen(s) counts the characters (without the '\\0').",
        "You can't copy strings with =. strcpy(dst, src) copies src into dst, and strcat(dst, src) adds src to the end of dst. dst must be big enough!",
        "You can't compare strings with == either. strcmp(a, b) returns 0 when they're equal, a negative number if a comes first in the alphabet, a positive one if b does."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "#include <string.h>",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    char name[20];",
          "    strcpy(name, \"Dennis\");",
          "    strcat(name, \" Ritchie\");",
          "    printf(\"%s (%d)\\n\", name, (int) strlen(name));",
          "    if (strcmp(name, \"Dennis Ritchie\") == 0)",
          "        printf(\"same\\n\");",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "Dennis Ritchie (14)",
          "same"
        ]
      },
